1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. Nf3 d6 6. d4 Nbd7 7. O-O e5 8. h3
c6 9. Bg5 h6 10. Bxf6 Qxf6 {White’s last and next moves, though they form a
system Kitto has practised with success, do not appeal to me. Black is left
with equal develop­ment and a better centre - more than he has any right, as
second player, to expect.} 11. dxe5 dxe5 12. Nd2 Qe7 13. f4 exf4 14. gxf4 Nc5 {
Already envisaging the excursion to d3 three moves, and the sacrifice four
moves, later. As I could not effectively prevent the clearly fore­shadowed e4,
I had to strive to exploit the fact that on that square the pawn temporarily
blocks the action of three white pieces.} 15. e4 Bd4+ 16. Kh2 Rd8 {Post-game
analysis revealed that my opponent was continually expecting me to complete my
development, in routine manner, by ...Be6 and a move of the a8-rook. But
always there was something better. Such games as this are snares for the
learner, they are an all too enticing exception — to the law "get all your
pieces out" — which prove the rule.} 17. Qe2 Nd3 $1 18. e5 ({Not} 18. Qxd3
Bg1+ 19. Kxg1 Rxd3) 18... Nxf4 $1 19. Rxf4 Qxe5 ({Not} 19... Bxe5 $4 20. Kh1 $1
) 20. Raf1 Bxc3 {This not only destroys the guard to White’s queen, but
keeps up the threat of ...g5 as well.} 21. Ne4 Bxb2 22. Nf6+ Kh8 23. Be4 Bc1 {
Sources: CHESS/20/230, October 1954, p31; BCM, November 1954, p359;
Illustrated London News, 2 October 1954} (23... Bc1 {For if} 24. Rxc1 {then}
Qxf4+ {comes. And, to cap it all, 24...Rd2 is threatened.}) 0-1

